**Goal:** decide where a piece of state lives on Cloudflare, and address it by name.

A Worker is Cloudflare's program that answers requests. It runs your code in an isolate, a lightweight sandbox inside the Workers runtime.
A Durable Object is a Worker with a globally unique name and its own storage. [What are Durable Objects?](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/concepts/what-are-durable-objects/)

## Why a Worker must not rely on module state

Module state can outlive a request, and that is the problem. Cloudflare lists three facts. [How Workers works](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/reference/how-workers-works/)

- One instance may handle many requests, including concurrent ones. State one request writes, another request can read.
- An isolate can be evicted, for example when the host runs short of resources. Its state goes with it.
- Nothing guarantees that two requests reach the same instance of your Worker.

So Cloudflare recommends that you do not use or mutate global state.
rat-stack fences that advice with the [`rat-stack-patterns/no-module-level-mutable-state`](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725/scripts/oxlint-plugin-patterns.ts) lint rule:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=scripts/oxlint-plugin-patterns.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=820-821 title="no-module-level-mutable-state"

```

The fix names three homes for state. This page covers the Durable Object.

## What a Durable Object adds

Cloudflare lists these properties. [What are Durable Objects?](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/concepts/what-are-durable-objects/)

- **A name:** you send a request to one specific object from anywhere.
- **Private storage:** transactional and strongly consistent, and no other object can read it.
- **One thread:** each object handles its work single-threaded, like code in a browser.
- **Created on first use:** you never create or destroy an object yourself.
- **In-memory state resets:** an idle object hibernates and loses memory. Persist anything you need later.
- **Alarms:** an object can schedule itself to wake up at a future time.

## Worked example: one object per join submission

The join intake keeps each submission's contact in its own `Interest` object.
The contact lives under one storage key:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/site/src/interest/join-contact-cell.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=3-3 title="One key per object"

```

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/site/src/interest/join-contact-cell.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=24-25 title="The cell reads that key from the storage it is given"

```

The `Interest` object hands the cell its own storage:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/site/src/interest/interest-durable-object.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=118-122 title="The cell is backed by the object's storage"

```

The Worker binds the namespaces once, in its init phase:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/site/src/worker.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=386-388 title="Bind the Durable Object namespaces"

```

Then it picks an object by name for each request:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=apps/site/src/worker.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=403-407 {407} title="One object per submission; one object for the index"

```

- ``interests.getByName(`join:${submissionId}`)`` reaches the object for one submission. Every request about that submission reaches the same object.
- `interestIndex.getByName("index")` reaches a single index object that lists applications.

The name is the design decision. One name per submission keeps each record apart. One fixed name makes a single place that every request shares.

## Choose the home

The lint fix names three homes: a `Ref` inside a [Layer](/lore/layers-make-dependencies-explicit), a Durable Object, or the database.
Pick a Durable Object when several clients must work through one named place. Cloudflare describes that use as coordination. [What are Durable Objects?](https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/concepts/what-are-durable-objects/)

Work that must finish after the response needs a recovery record too. See [Request lifetime is not durability](/lore/request-lifetime-is-not-durability).

